from (values
('127.677.d.9.F',3,'An incident response plan should clearly list:','["employee birthdays", "vacation schedules", "roles, contact information and steps to follow", "only the company slogan"]'::jsonb,2,'Plans define who does what.','original'),
('127.677.d.9.F',4,'Why should an incident response plan be tested and updated regularly?','["testing wastes evidence", "plans never need changes", "updates cancel the plan", "threats and systems change over time"]'::jsonb,3,'Plans must stay current.','original'),
('127.677.d.9.G',3,'A user who keeps using a computer after an incident can compromise evidence by:','["overwriting or altering data", "creating backups of evidence", "protecting logs", "improving timestamps"]'::jsonb,0,'Continued use changes the system.','original'),
('127.677.d.9.G',4,'Which action could damage the validity of evidence on a suspect computer?','["isolating it from the network", "browsing files and installing programs on it", "photographing the screen", "labeling it for storage"]'::jsonb,1,'Interacting with the system alters data.','original'),
('127.677.d.10.A',3,'The documented record of everyone who possessed an item of evidence is called the:','["evidence index", "case summary", "chain of custody", "handling invoice"]'::jsonb,2,'The chain of custody tracks possession.','original'),
('127.677.d.10.A',4,'Each time evidence changes hands, the chain of custody form should be updated with:','["the building''s address", "the evidence color", "a new case title", "the date, time and names of the people involved"]'::jsonb,3,'Transfers must be recorded.','original'),
('127.677.d.10.B',3,'Safe handling of digital evidence includes:','["using anti-static bags and labeling items", "stacking devices on a heater", "carrying drives loose in a pocket", "leaving them in a car"]'::jsonb,0,'Proper packaging protects evidence.','original'),
('127.677.d.10.B',4,'To handle sensitive data safely, an investigator should store evidence in:','["a shared hallway", "a locked, access-controlled location", "a public locker", "an open desk"]'::jsonb,1,'Secure storage limits access.','original'),
('127.677.d.10.C',3,'Which security goal ensures that information is available when authorized users need it?','["non-repudiation", "integrity", "availability", "confidentiality"]'::jsonb,2,'Availability keeps systems usable.','original'),
('127.677.d.10.C',4,'Non-repudiation means a person cannot:','["change their password", "use a computer", "access a network", "deny having performed an action"]'::jsonb,3,'Proof prevents denial.','original'),
('127.677.d.10.D',3,'If two logs show different times for the same event, the investigator should:','["note the discrepancy and investigate the cause", "delete both", "pick whichever is shorter", "ignore one log"]'::jsonb,0,'Conflicts must be resolved with evidence.','original'),
('127.677.d.10.D',4,'An employee who notices unusual login alerts should:','["delete the alerts", "report them to the security team promptly", "post them on social media", "keep quiet"]'::jsonb,1,'Prompt reporting enables response.','original'),
('127.677.d.10.E',3,'While reviewing network traffic, an event of interest might be:','["a normal software update", "a printer status check", "a connection to a known malicious address", "a video call to a coworker"]'::jsonb,2,'Known bad destinations stand out.','original'),
('127.677.d.10.E',4,'A sudden spike in DNS requests for random-looking domain names may indicate:','["a monitor failure", "a power outage", "a healthy network", "malware communicating with its controller"]'::jsonb,3,'Generated domains are a malware sign.','original'),
('127.677.d.10.F',3,'In an event log, repeated failed logins followed by a success from the same account may indicate:','["a successful password guessing attack", "a printer test", "routine behavior", "a screen update"]'::jsonb,0,'Persistence then success suggests guessing.','original'),
('127.677.d.10.F',4,'A security log entry showing a new administrator account created at an unusual hour is:','["always harmless", "an event of interest worth investigating", "a backup confirmation", "a system font change"]'::jsonb,1,'Unexpected admin creation is suspicious.','original'),
('127.677.d.11.A',3,'An attack signature is:','["a monitor setting", "a file''s color", "a recognizable pattern that identifies a known attack", "a person''s handwriting"]'::jsonb,2,'Signatures match known patterns.','original'),
('127.677.d.11.A',4,'Signature-based detection has trouble catching:','["old worms with signatures", "well-documented exploits", "known viruses", "brand-new attacks with no known pattern"]'::jsonb,3,'New attacks lack signatures.','original'),
('127.677.d.11.B',3,'A route an attacker uses to reach a system, such as an email attachment, is an:','["attack vector", "firewall rule", "backup route", "security patch"]'::jsonb,0,'A vector is the path of attack.','original'),
('127.677.d.11.B',4,'Forging a sender''s address to make an email look trustworthy is called:','["encrypting", "spoofing", "archiving", "defragmenting"]'::jsonb,1,'Spoofing disguises the origin.','original'),
('127.677.d.11.C',3,'An attack that uses several steps, such as phishing, then malware, then data theft, is:','["single-step", "hardware-only", "multistage", "accidental"]'::jsonb,2,'Multistage attacks chain actions together.','original'),
('127.677.d.11.C',4,'Multistage attacks are harder to stop because defenders must detect:','["only the final step", "nothing before the end", "only one login", "activity across several phases over time"]'::jsonb,3,'Pieces may look harmless alone.','original'),
('127.678.d.1.A',3,'A personal career plan for a cybersecurity job should list:','["target roles, needed education and skills to build", "random hobbies", "only favorite foods", "pet names"]'::jsonb,0,'Plans connect goals to the steps needed.','original'),
('127.678.d.1.A',4,'Setting short-term and long-term career goals helps a student:','["avoid all planning", "map steps such as certifications and internships", "ignore job requirements", "postpone every decision"]'::jsonb,1,'Goals turn into actionable steps.','original'),
('127.678.d.1.B',3,'A resume for a security analyst role should highlight:','["pets'' names", "unrelated hobbies only", "relevant skills, projects and certifications", "a long list of favorite shows"]'::jsonb,2,'Resumes should match the job.','original'),
('127.678.d.1.B',4,'A portfolio for an aspiring penetration tester might include:','["private customer passwords", "stolen data", "exploit code used without permission", "write-ups of lab exercises and capture-the-flag challenges"]'::jsonb,3,'Lawful practice work shows skill.','original'),
('127.678.d.1.C',3,'Before a job interview, a candidate should:','["research the company and practice answers", "memorize nothing", "arrive late", "avoid preparing"]'::jsonb,0,'Preparation improves performance.','original'),
('127.678.d.1.C',4,'When an interviewer asks about a weakness, a strong answer:','["claims to have none", "names a real weakness and the steps taken to improve it", "changes the subject", "blames a former boss"]'::jsonb,1,'Honesty plus growth impresses interviewers.','original'),
('127.678.d.2.A',3,'A hospital loses patient records in a breach. Which U.S. law about health information privacy is most relevant?','["COPPA", "FERPA", "HIPAA", "GLBA"]'::jsonb,2,'HIPAA governs health information.','original'),
('127.678.d.2.A',4,'A school wants to share a student''s grades with a third party. Which U.S. law protects education records?','["CFAA", "HIPAA", "DMCA", "FERPA"]'::jsonb,3,'FERPA protects student education records.','original'),
('127.678.d.2.B',3,'The 2020 SolarWinds incident is best described as a:','["supply chain attack", "lost laptop", "printer error", "phishing text only"]'::jsonb,0,'Attackers compromised trusted software updates.','original'),
('127.678.d.2.B',4,'The Equifax breach exposed sensitive data for roughly how many people in the U.S.?','["about 1,000", "about 147 million", "about 2 million", "about 50,000"]'::jsonb,1,'Equifax reported roughly 147 million affected.','original'),
('127.678.d.2.C',3,'Section 508 of the Rehabilitation Act requires federal technology to be:','["written in one language", "only used on paper", "accessible to people with disabilities", "free of charge"]'::jsonb,2,'Section 508 addresses accessibility.','original'),
('127.678.d.2.C',4,'Making a website compatible with screen readers supports compliance with:','["maritime law", "tax rules", "zoning codes", "accessibility requirements"]'::jsonb,3,'Screen reader support is an accessibility feature.','original'),
('127.678.d.3.A',3,'Which statement reflects the tension between privacy and security?','["monitoring users can improve security but reduces their privacy", "privacy and security are always identical", "privacy requires no rules", "security never needs data"]'::jsonb,0,'Security measures often collect personal data.','original'),
('127.678.d.3.A',4,'A supporter of strong encryption might argue that it:','["is illegal everywhere", "protects user privacy even if it makes some investigations harder", "has no effect on privacy", "makes data public"]'::jsonb,1,'Encryption trades investigative access for privacy.','original'),
('127.678.d.3.B',3,'A security researcher finds a flaw and tells the vendor privately. This is generally:','["illegal in all cases", "required to be public at once", "ethical, responsible disclosure", "unethical blackmail"]'::jsonb,2,'Private reporting lets vendors fix flaws.','original'),
('127.678.d.3.B',4,'Which action is unethical for a penetration tester?','["reporting vulnerabilities to the client", "following the rules of engagement", "documenting findings", "testing systems outside the agreed scope"]'::jsonb,3,'Staying in scope is an ethical duty.','original'),
('127.678.d.4.A',3,'Which sequence lists the penetration testing phases in order?','["plan, discover, attack, report", "discover, report, plan, attack", "report, attack, discover, plan", "attack, report, plan, discover"]'::jsonb,0,'Testers plan, discover, attack, then report.','original'),
('127.678.d.4.A',4,'In which phase does the tester gather information such as open ports and services?','["plan", "discover", "report", "attack"]'::jsonb,1,'Discovery maps the target.','original'),
('127.678.d.4.B',3,'Before starting a penetration test, the tester must obtain:','["nothing", "a competitor''s approval", "written authorization from the system owner", "a verbal hint"]'::jsonb,2,'Authorization makes testing legal.','original'),
('127.678.d.4.B',4,'The agreement that defines what a tester may and may not do is the:','["marketing plan", "user manual", "press release", "rules of engagement"]'::jsonb,3,'Rules of engagement set the scope.','original'),
('127.678.d.4.C',3,'A tool that checks which network ports are open on a host performs:','["port scanning", "disk cleanup", "photo editing", "file backup"]'::jsonb,0,'Port scans reveal listening services.','original'),
('127.678.d.4.C',4,'Which tool can scan a web server for known vulnerabilities?','["a text editor", "a vulnerability scanner such as Nessus", "a spreadsheet program", "a presentation program"]'::jsonb,1,'Scanners check for known flaws.','original'),
('127.678.d.4.D',3,'A scan finds an outdated web server version. A next step is to:','["ignore the result", "uninstall the scanner", "research known exploits for that version", "delete the server"]'::jsonb,2,'Version information points to known flaws.','original'),
('127.678.d.4.D',4,'Matching scan results to databases of known flaws, such as CVE entries, helps a tester:','["avoid authorization", "skip the report", "hide the findings", "identify which exploits might work"]'::jsonb,3,'CVE entries link versions to exploits.','original'),
('127.678.d.4.E',3,'When prioritizing fixes after a scan, which vulnerability should be addressed first?','["a critical flaw on an internet-facing server", "a minor setting on an isolated PC", "a cosmetic typo", "a low-risk issue on an unused test machine"]'::jsonb,0,'High severity and exposure come first.','original'),
('127.678.d.4.E',4,'Prioritization of mitigations usually weighs severity against:','["parking space number", "likelihood of exploitation and asset value", "employee shoe size", "office color"]'::jsonb,1,'Risk depends on likelihood and value.','original'),
('127.678.d.5.A',3,'Advanced Encryption Standard (AES) is a:','["hashing function", "firewall rule", "symmetric encryption algorithm", "asymmetric key exchange only"]'::jsonb,2,'AES uses one shared key.','original'),
('127.678.d.5.A',4,'RSA is an example of:','["symmetric encryption", "a substitution cipher", "a hash function", "asymmetric encryption"]'::jsonb,3,'RSA uses public and private keys.','original'),
('127.678.d.5.B',3,'A hash function produces a fixed-length output called a:','["digest", "cipher", "token ring", "key pair"]'::jsonb,0,'Hash outputs are digests.','original'),
('127.678.d.5.B',4,'In a blockchain, each block contains the hash of the:','["operating system", "previous block", "next block", "user password"]'::jsonb,1,'Hash links chain the blocks.','original'),
('127.678.d.5.C',3,'Password salting adds:','["a second login page", "extra spaces after typing", "random data to a password before hashing", "a longer username"]'::jsonb,2,'Salts make each hash unique.','original'),
('127.678.d.5.C',4,'Salting helps defend against:','["slow printers", "power surges", "screen glare", "precomputed rainbow table attacks"]'::jsonb,3,'Unique salts defeat precomputed tables.','original'),
('127.678.d.5.D',3,'A digital signature is created using the sender''s:','["private key", "public key", "IP address", "email address"]'::jsonb,0,'Only the sender holds the private key.','original')
